{
  "_comment": [
    "Which columns of the published data scripts/field_gaps.py checks for gaps, and how empty each may be.",
    "A ceiling is the largest share of rows (0 to 1) that may be empty: blank, or one of blank_values. It was set from the data on the day the column was added, plus 0.02, so it flags a column getting worse. A column that was never empty has a ceiling of 0.",
    "min_rows is a floor on the row count (95% of the count that day), so a truncated file is a gap too.",
    "List only columns a reader relies on. A column that is sparse by design (moratorium_id, opposition_groups) does not belong here.",
    "When the data improves, tighten: python scripts/field_gaps.py --suggest prints this file with every ceiling recomputed. When a rise is expected, raise the ceiling and say why in the pull request."
  ],
